--- main.maude: the entry point of the TEE formal specification, with every feature on.
---
---   maude src/main.maude                  load the specification
---   make NAME=t CRYPTO=0                  build/t/src/main.maude without the crypto APIs
---
--- make (see Makefile and features.mk) filters this file with tools/strip-modules.py
--- and writes the result to build/<NAME>/src/main.maude, next to the selected spec files.
--- With a feature off, its files lose their load lines and its modules their pr lines; the
--- feature that owns each file is noted in brackets below, and files without a note are
--- always loaded.  The filter rules:
---   * only active lines count: a line that starts with load, or with pr in a module;
---   * a comment whose text is itself a load or pr command is removed from the output, so
---     disabled code must not be kept here as a comment;
---   * every other comment line, such as this one, is copied unchanged.
---
--- The top modules
---   TEE-BEHAVIOR       the TEE system: the TrustedOS, TA instances and their kernels,
---                      sessions, rich applications (RAs), the storage and cryptographic
---                      object APIs of the GlobalPlatform TEE Internal Core API (chapters 5
---                      and 6), the panics of programmer errors, and shared memory.
---   TEE-C-BEHAVIOR   the program side: how a call written in a TA or RA program, in the
---                      language of lang/c/semantics.maude, becomes a TEE API call and gets
---                      its result back: lang/c/tee-support.maude and the *-TEE-CALL modules.
---   TEE-RUST-BEHAVIOR  the Stable MIR program side and its transient-object API calls;
---                      import with TEE-BEHAVIOR to run Rust TAs.
--- A model that runs programs imports both; a model that starts from API calls written
--- directly in the configuration, api-call : init(...), needs only TEE-BEHAVIOR.
---
--- A configuration is a multiset of objects and messages:
---   < ra : RichApp | prog : P, proc : K >          an RA running the program P
---   < tos : TrustedOS | ta-config : ..., ... >     the OS, with the program of each TA
---   < sessionId(N) : Session | source : ..., destination : ..., state : ... >
---   < oid(N, tos) : TrustedApp | prog : P, ... >   a TA instance, created by the OS
---   < oid(N, ta) : TransObj | ... >, ObjHandle, PersistObj, Storage, ...  TEE objects
---   msg r[args] from O to O'                       a message in flight
---
--- main.maude runs no commands, because every test file and analysis model loads it.  The
--- sample commands at the end of this file are comments: load main.maude in Maude and paste
--- them at the prompt.

--- Values, data and the imperative language
load constants                   --- object types, usages, flags, algorithms, return codes
load data                        --- abstract data of data streams and buffers
load tee-data-type               --- API structures such as TEE_ObjectInfo
load k-prelude                   --- the K-style configuration of program execution
load tee-api                     --- the sort of TEE API names
load lang                        --- what the model shares with the program language

--- Objects, handles and messages
load objects                     --- object classes of the storage and crypto APIs
load handle                      --- opaque handles (object, operation, session, enumerator)
load tee-msg                     --- messages msg r[args] from sender to receiver
load tee-api-call                --- values carried by TEE API calls

--- The TEE system: OS, applications, sessions
load os                          --- the TrustedOS, TA configurations, TA invocation
load app                         --- the App class shared by TAs and RAs
load tee-kernel                  --- the TA kernel and its API call state
load tee-app                     --- the TrustedApp class and its commands
load os-service                  --- the services of the TrustedOS
load session                     --- open, invoke and close of client-TA sessions

--- Storage object APIs (GP Internal Core API, chapter 5)
load transient-object            --- TransObj class, shared by the other storage files
load transient                   --- transient object APIs              [TRANSIENT]
load persistent-object           --- PersistObj class, shared by the other storage files
load persistent                  --- persistent object APIs             [PERSISTENT]
load data-stream                 --- data stream access APIs            [DATA_STREAM]
load generic                     --- generic object APIs                [GENERIC]
load enumerator                  --- persistent object enumeration      [ENUMERATOR]

--- Cryptographic operation APIs (chapter 6)
load cryptographic               --- operations, keys, cipher, MAC, digest  [CRYPTO]

--- Panics, the REE side, shared memory
load tee-panic                   --- panics of programmer errors and invalid handles
load ree-app                     --- the RichApp class and the TEE Client API calls
load shared-memory               --- memory references of TEEC_InvokeCommand  [SHM]

--- The language of the programs (lang/c): its TEE API calls, client calls and program runs.
load ../lang/c/semantics         --- syntax and semantics of the TA/RA language
load ../lang/c/typing            --- static type checking (wellTyped) of its programs
load ../lang/c/tee-support       --- program calls translated into TEE API calls

--- Stable MIR programs and their TEE API boundary (import TEE-RUST-BEHAVIOR to run them).
load ../lang/rust/semantics
load ../lang/rust/tee-support

--- TEE-BEHAVIOR: the rewrite rules of the TEE system and of every API group.  Each pr line
--- of a feature module is dropped by the build when the feature is off.

--- @kind assembly
--- @feature core
mod TEE-BEHAVIOR is
  pr TA-KERNEL-BEHAVIOR .
  pr SESSION-BEHAVIOR .
  pr GENERIC-OBJECT-BEHAVIOR .
  pr TRANSIENT-OBJECT-BEHAVIOR .
  pr PERSISTENT-OBJECT-BEHAVIOR .
  pr DATA-STREAM-OBJECT-BEHAVIOR .
  pr ENUMERATOR-OBJECT-BEHAVIOR .
  pr CRYPTO-OPERATION-BEHAVIOR .
  pr TRUST-APP-BEHAVIOR .
  pr TRUST-APP-CALL-BEHAVIOR .
  pr RICH-APP-BEHAVIOR .
  pr GENERAL-PANIC .
  pr PERSISTENT-PANIC .
  pr DATA-STREAM-PANIC .
  pr CRYPTO-PANIC .
endm

--- TEE-C-BEHAVIOR: the TEE API calls of programs (one *-TEE-CALL module per API group),
--- the calls of a TA to other TAs, and the program execution of a TA.

--- @kind assembly
--- @feature c
mod TEE-C-BEHAVIOR is
  pr TRANSIENT-OBJECT-TEE-CALL .
  pr PERSISTENT-OBJECT-TEE-CALL .
  pr GENERIC-OBJECT-TEE-CALL .
  pr DATA-STREAM-OBJECT-TEE-CALL .
  pr ENUMERATOR-OBJECT-TEE-CALL .
  pr CRYPTO-OPERATION-TEE-CALL .
  pr TEE-INTERNAL-CLIENT-CALL .
  pr TRUST-APP-C-BEHAVIOR .
  pr RICH-APP-C-BEHAVIOR .
  pr SHARED-OBJECT-C .
  pr SHARED-MEMORY-BEHAVIOR .
endm

--- MAIN-SAMPLE: the scenario of the sample commands below.  A rich application ra opens a
--- session with the TA SAMPLE-TA, invokes its command keyCheck, and closes the session.
--- keyCheck calls TEE_AllocateTransientObject for an AES key of N bits: on success it copies
--- the input buffer of the RA to the output, and on an error it writes noData to the output.
--- The TrustedOS tos creates the TA instance when the session is opened; the initial
--- configuration init(N, F) has no TA instance.  The RA calls teecInvokeCommand, so a build
--- with SHM=0 cuts this module out.  [SHM]
--- @kind program
--- @feature shm
mod MAIN-SAMPLE is
  pr TEE-BEHAVIOR .
  pr TEE-C-BEHAVIOR .

  op ra : -> Oid [ctor] .
  op tos : -> Oid [ctor] .
  op SAMPLE-TA : -> AppId [ctor] .
  op keyCheck : -> FuncId [ctor] .

  var N : Nat .
  var F : FuncId .

  --- The TA program: the command keyCheck.  buf points to the input object of the client,
  --- and out is the output object; both are shared memory of the RA.  Quoted identifiers
  --- such as 'rr are program variables.
  op taProg : Nat -> Program .
  eq taProg(N) =
    void keyCheck(char * 'buf, char & 'out) {
      TEE-Result 'rr ; TEE-ObjectHandle 'h ;
      'rr := allocateTransientObject(# typeAes, # N, 'h) ;
      if ('rr == # success) { 'out := deref('buf) ; } else { 'out := # noData ; }
      return unit ; } .

  --- The RA program: open a session, invoke F with the locations of b and out, close.
  --- r1, r2 and r3 get the return codes of the three TEE Client API calls.
  op raProg : FuncId -> Program .
  eq raProg(F) =
    TEEC-Session 's ; char 'b ; char 'out ; TEE-Result 'r1 ; TEE-Result 'r2 ; TEE-Result 'r3 ;
    'b := # data(1) ; 'out := # noData ;
    'r1 := teecOpenSession(# SAMPLE-TA, 's) ;
    'r2 := teecInvokeCommand('s, # F, (addr('b), addr('out))) ;
    'r3 := teecCloseSession('s) ; .

  --- init(N, F): the RA runs raProg(F); SAMPLE-TA is a single-instance TA with one session
  --- at a time, taConfig(P, true, false), whose program allocates an N-bit AES key.
  op init : Nat FuncId -> Configuration .
  eq init(N, F) =
    < ra : RichApp | prog : raProg(F), proc : mkConf(raProg(F)) >
    < tos : TrustedOS | session-counter : 0,
        ta-config : (SAMPLE-TA |-> taConfig(taProg(N), true, false)),
        ta-instances : empty, ta-storages : empty > .
endm

--- Sample commands
---
--- They run on the scenario of the module MAIN-SAMPLE above; init(N, keyCheck) is its
--- initial configuration.  Load main.maude
--- and paste a command at the Maude prompt, without the leading "---"; each prints its
--- result in a few milliseconds.
---
--- 1. One run to the end.  In the result, the store of ra holds sessionId(0) for s, data(1)
---    for out, and success for r1, r2 and r3; the TA instance oid(0, tos) holds the
---    transient object it allocated.
---
---   rew in MAIN-SAMPLE : init(128, keyCheck) .
---
--- 2. All final states.  The run is deterministic up to interleaving: one final state.
---
---   search in MAIN-SAMPLE : init(128, keyCheck) =>! C:Configuration .
---
--- 3. A pattern on the final state: the RA has finished, and the TA copied data(1) to out.
---    LO and L2 are the locations of the RA variables out and r2 in its store.
---
---   search [1] in MAIN-SAMPLE : init(128, keyCheck) =>!
---     < ra : RichApp | proc : (k(.K)
---         env(('out |-> LO:Nat) ; ('r2 |-> L2:Nat) ; ENV:Map{Var,Nat})
---         store((LO:Nat |-> # data(1)) ; (L2:Nat |-> # success) ; MEM:Map{Nat,Value?})
---         KS:KConfig), AS:AttributeSet >
---     C:Configuration .
---
--- 4. The error path: a 64-bit AES key is not supported, so allocateTransientObject returns
---    TEE_ERROR_NOT_SUPPORTED and keyCheck writes noData to out.  The invocation itself
---    still succeeds: r2 is success.  Maude does not reduce search patterns, so the pattern
---    writes noData in its normal form, # (nil).List{Data}.
---
---   search [1] in MAIN-SAMPLE : init(64, keyCheck) =>!
---     < ra : RichApp | proc : (k(.K)
---         env(('out |-> LO:Nat) ; ('r2 |-> L2:Nat) ; ENV:Map{Var,Nat})
---         store((LO:Nat |-> # (nil).List{Data}) ; (L2:Nat |-> # success) ; MEM:Map{Nat,Value?})
---         KS:KConfig), AS:AttributeSet >
---     C:Configuration .
---
--- 5. An invariant over every reachable state: no TA ever panics.  Expected: No solution.
---
---   search in MAIN-SAMPLE : init(128, keyCheck) =>*
---     < O:Oid : TrustedApp | status : panic, AS:AttributeSet > C:Configuration .
---
--- 6. A reachable intermediate state: the session is invoking, that is, the TA is serving
---    the command of the RA.
---
---   search [1] in MAIN-SAMPLE : init(128, keyCheck) =>*
---     < S:SessionId : Session | source : ra, state : invoking, AS:AttributeSet >
---     C:Configuration .
---
--- To see how a state was reached, take its number from a search result and run, e.g.,
---   show path labels 34 .
--- Other runs: init(256, keyCheck), or edit taProg in MAIN-SAMPLE to call other TEE
--- APIs; lang/c/tee-support.maude lists their program signatures, and tests/completion/*.maude has
--- more scenarios.
